;; The reference set `bb perf` gates against — the performance counterpart of
;; the committed visual baselines under tools/visual-tests/**-snapshots/.
;; Committed, reviewed in the diff, refreshed only by an explicit
;; `bb perf-update` that a human then commits.
;;
;; GENERATED by `bb perf-update` — edit :why freely (it is preserved), but this
;; header is re-emitted from scripts/perf.clj on every write.
;;
;; Every entry is a COUNT of a structural event, never a duration. That is the
;; whole design, not a simplification: `1` is `1` on a laptop, on ubuntu-latest,
;; and at the load average 75 that scripts/ci.clj once measured while timing out
;; 9 of 17 checks. docs/PERF_NOTES.md records a genuine optimisation measuring
;; "~5% — inside the run's std-dev"; counts have no std-dev. Wall-clock numbers
;; live in perf/runs/*.edn and are reported, never gated.
;;
;; ONLY invariants belong here — counters that do not move when somebody adds a
;; test. `:registry/delta-recompile` (346 across the unit suite) and
;; `:registry/rebuild` (107) grow with the namespace count; gating them would
;; fail honest PRs and train everyone to re-baseline without reading, which is
;; how a gate becomes a rubber stamp. They are reported as trend data instead.
;;
;; :max is a CEILING. Exceeding it fails `bb perf`, and the :why is what gets
;; printed — write it for whoever sees the failure with no context.

{:unit
 {:fixture/container-boot
  {:max 1,
   :why
   "A second PostgreSQL container means some caller bypassed get-container's double-checked lock. Costs ~3 s and breaks the per-NS logical-DB isolation the whole shared-container design rests on."},
  :fixture/golden-bootstrap
  {:max 3,
   :why
   "The golden bootstrap is ~14 s and is cached per JVM x package-set. THREE are sanctioned: the default `[core web app]` golden most suites clone; ONE `[core web app registry mcp]` golden shared by the suites that exercise the OPTIONAL `registry` / `mcp` packages (registry-test, export-test, mcp-endpoint-test) — `app` no longer pulls those packages, so those suites must ask for the full set; and ONE cheap `[core]` golden (~3 s — no web/app sync, tiny compile) for NSes that exercise a single core primitive and were paying a full-bundle eager compile for it (resolver-binding-test first; migrate other pure-core NSes onto it rather than minting new sets). A FOURTH means a suite asked for yet another package set nobody else uses — check its `packages` vector before accepting the cost."},
  :fixture/type-check-sweep
  {:max 0,
   :why
   "The topological type-check sweep is ~40 s, the single most expensive fixture step there is. The unit suite has never needed one. If this fires, a namespace started bootstrapping with :skip-type-check? false — that is what 6840f542 removed."},
  :registry/delta-fell-back-to-rebuild
  {:max 0,
   :why
   "A caller named its changed fn-ids and STILL paid for a full graph recompile: delta-recompile! hit a cold reverse-deps index and silently called rebuild!. Nothing in the return value says so and no timing distinguishes it from a cold cache — this counter is the only evidence. It is the exact failure 9d66adc0 and 0b74f1dc were about; measured at 4137 fns, the rebuild it hides costs 49.8 s."},
  :registry/delta-read-graph
  {:max 26,
   :why
   "A delta recompile (or a type-registry refresh) that read the WHOLE graph out of Postgres instead of taking the primed :graph-cache. The cache is the full org-agnostic graph on every deployment, so only a cold cache or a sharded (:executor-orgs) pod should ever read here. On the cloud that read was 1.7 s of a 1.85 s merge and ran on EVERY write (2026-09-03) — a timing could not tell it from a slow database. 26 = the suite's cold-cache cases at the time the counter was added."}},
 :perf
 #:sql{:graph-entities-tree
       {:max 1,
        :why
        "The sidebar's first paint. 1 round trip, steady-state (the compile is paid by whichever scenario runs first — see bb.edn's test-perf note on --no-randomize). This endpoint was 95 ms / 4.5 MB before it was scoped down to ?scope=tree; if this climbs, the scoping has sprung a leak and the sidebar is reading rows it does not paint."},
       :create-fn
       {:max 24,
        :why
        "Creating one :fn costs 24 round trips, steady-state — it runs first, so it pays the compile reads for the suite. 19→20 (2026-07-25): the graph-epoch bump — one nextval BEFORE every graph-shaped write, the freshness self-heal's write half (storage.postgres.graph-epoch); deliberate. 20→24 (2026-08-27): the bare :fn create now runs the post-write type-check so a freshly-extended child records its rich-types entry (typed repr / fn-return-type / effects work before the first binding) — reconstruct-fn-def reads the fn + parents + ns rows; deliberate. A further jump means the write path started re-reading what it already had: this operation once spent 477 ms of its 918 ms re-reading the whole graph to recompile a closure whose blast radius was one fn (9c1b882a spliced the cache instead)."},
       :list-secrets
       {:max 8,
        :why
        "GET /api/secrets — 6 round trips steady-state, INDEPENDENT of how big the graph is, and deliberately budgeted with headroom rather than tightly. This one number jitters by ±1 with branch-row caching (measured 5, 6 and 7 across runs on two machines), so a tight ceiling fails honestly-green runs and a loose one misses small regressions. The real guard is in the scenario itself: pg_stat_statements normalises a graph scan into one row with calls > 1, and the test asserts every statement runs exactly once. This budget is the coarse backstop for a change that adds whole new statements — cross 8 and something structural moved."},
       :merge-fork
       {:max 35,
        :why
        "POST /api/branches/main/merge for a branch forked off main carrying one new fn — lesson 20's flow, every review's last click. Before 2026-09-03 the merge ran a full resolved-view diff of both branches (`untransferable-inherited-entities`) and scanned EVERY version row on main for conflicts — thousands of round trips' worth of rows, 1.6 s here and ~7 s on the cloud. The fork case now decides from the two branches' visibility sets (a fork shows nothing the target can't see) and the conflict scan asks the target only about the ids the source touched. If this climbs by more than a handful, somebody put a whole-branch read back on the merge path."},
       :execute-popover-app-root
       {:max 14,
        :why
        "GET /partials/execute-popover for `web-server` — the fn whose reachable closure is the whole application. Its free-arg walk loads that closure through the storage's own graph resolver (a constant handful of round trips) and walks it in memory with per-fn memoisation. Before 2026-09-02 the walk was a per-level BFS — four queries per level, dozens of levels — and the Run form for the app root took 30–50 s (the inspector's Runs tab looked hung). 18 → 15 → 14 on 2026-09-06: the resolver's four entity loads share one `branch_merge` read (the request-scoped merge-record memo), and the scenario now runs the handler under that request scope the way the router does. If this number grows with the DEPTH of the app graph, somebody put a query back inside the walk; +1…+4 means the memo scope was lost."},
       :graph-layout
       {:max 0,
        :why
        "POST /api/graph/layout — every card expansion in the editor, and steady-state it costs ZERO round trips: the layout reads the graph the compile cache already holds. A budget of 0 is the strictest one in this file and the cheapest to reason about — the FIRST query this endpoint issues is the regression, no factor to argue about. If it fires, a caching change started re-reading the graph per expansion."}},
